## Requirements

Assume: multiple floors, spot sizes SMALL/MEDIUM/LARGE, vehicle types MOTORCYCLE/CAR/TRUCK, hourly pricing, entry returns a ticket, exit takes a ticket and returns a fee. Out of scope: real payments, persistence, physical gates.

## Entities and relationships
- `ParkingLot` is the facade and owns floors (composition).
- `Floor` owns spots.
- `Spot` has an id, a size and an occupant.
- `Ticket` links a vehicle, a spot and an entry time.
- `PricingStrategy` and `SpotAllocator` are interfaces, so rates and allocation order can change independently.

## Design in TypeScript

```ts
type VehicleType = "MOTORCYCLE" | "CAR" | "TRUCK";
type SpotSize = "SMALL" | "MEDIUM" | "LARGE";

const fits: Record<VehicleType, SpotSize[]> = {
  MOTORCYCLE: ["SMALL", "MEDIUM", "LARGE"],
  CAR: ["MEDIUM", "LARGE"],
  TRUCK: ["LARGE"],
};

class Spot {
  occupied = false;
  constructor(readonly id: string, readonly size: SpotSize, readonly floor: number) {}
  canFit(v: VehicleType) { return !this.occupied && fits[v].includes(this.size); }
}

interface SpotAllocator { find(floors: Floor[], v: VehicleType): Spot | null }
interface PricingStrategy { fee(entry: Date, exit: Date): number }

class HourlyPricing implements PricingStrategy {
  constructor(private ratePerHour: number) {}
  fee(entry: Date, exit: Date) {
    const hours = Math.max(1, Math.ceil((exit.getTime() - entry.getTime()) / 3_600_000));
    return hours * this.ratePerHour;
  }
}

class Ticket {
  constructor(readonly id: string, readonly plate: string, readonly spot: Spot, readonly entry: Date) {}
}

class ParkingLot {
  private active = new Map<string, Ticket>();
  constructor(private floors: Floor[], private alloc: SpotAllocator,
              private pricing: PricingStrategy, private now: () => Date = () => new Date()) {}

  park(plate: string, type: VehicleType): Ticket {
    const spot = this.alloc.find(this.floors, type);
    if (!spot) throw new Error("LOT_FULL");
    spot.occupied = true;
    const t = new Ticket(crypto.randomUUID(), plate, spot, this.now());
    this.active.set(t.id, t);
    return t;
  }

  unpark(ticketId: string): number {
    const t = this.active.get(ticketId);
    if (!t) throw new Error("INVALID_TICKET");
    const fee = this.pricing.fee(t.entry, this.now());
    t.spot.occupied = false;
    this.active.delete(ticketId);
    return fee;
  }
}
```

`Floor` is just `{ spots: Spot[] }` plus helpers. The clock is injected (`now`) so tests are deterministic.

## Allocation strategies
- First-free (nearest to entrance): scan floors in order, O(spots). Fine for a few hundred spots.
- Per-size free lists: keep a stack of free spot ids per size, so allocation is O(1). Needed if the lot has tens of thousands of spots.
- Fallback to larger sizes if exact fit is unavailable, but track it so a truck is not blocked by cars in large spots.

## Concurrency
JavaScript is single-threaded within one process, so the code above is atomic between awaits. If `find` and `occupied = true` are split by an `await` (database call), two requests can pick the same spot. Fix with a per-lot mutex, a compare-and-set on the spot (`UPDATE ... WHERE occupied = false`), or one queue per floor.

## Extensions interviewers ask
- Multiple entry/exit gates: the lot is shared; add the lock above.
- Pricing by vehicle type or peak hours: new `PricingStrategy`, no change to `ParkingLot`.
- EV charging spots: add a `features` set to Spot and match on it.
- Display board of free counts: Observer on spot state changes.
- Lost ticket: fall back to plate lookup via a `Map<plate, Ticket>`.

## Pitfalls
- A subclass per vehicle with identical behaviour.
- Putting fee logic inside Ticket or Spot.
- Using `Date.now()` directly so fees cannot be tested.

lld-method:3.body:
## Problem

A building has N floors and M elevators. People press a hall button (floor and up/down) or a cab button (destination floor). Design the system so requests are served efficiently and nobody starves.

## Entities
- `Elevator`: current floor, direction, state, pending stops.
- `ElevatorController` (dispatcher): receives hall calls and picks an elevator.
- `Request`: hall call `{floor, direction}` or cab call `{elevatorId, floor}`.
- `Direction`: UP, DOWN, IDLE.

## Scheduling: SCAN / LOOK
Serving requests first-come-first-served makes the car zig-zag. The classic improvement mirrors disk scheduling: keep moving in the current direction, stopping at every requested floor on the way, then reverse when nothing remains ahead (LOOK; SCAN would go to the end of the shaft). Stops live in two sorted sets: `upStops` (floors above) and `downStops` (floors below).

```ts
type Dir = "UP" | "DOWN" | "IDLE";

class Elevator {
  floor = 0;
  dir: Dir = "IDLE";
  private up = new Set<number>();    // stops above, served ascending
  private down = new Set<number>();  // stops below, served descending
  constructor(readonly id: number) {}

  addStop(f: number) {
    if (f === this.floor) return;
    (f > this.floor ? this.up : this.down).add(f);
    if (this.dir === "IDLE") this.dir = f > this.floor ? "UP" : "DOWN";
  }

  // one simulation tick: move a floor or serve a stop
  step() {
    if (this.dir === "UP") {
      const next = Math.min(...this.up);
      this.floor++;
      if (this.floor === next) this.up.delete(next);
      if (this.up.size === 0) this.dir = this.down.size ? "DOWN" : "IDLE";
    } else if (this.dir === "DOWN") {
      const next = Math.max(...this.down);
      this.floor--;
      if (this.floor === next) this.down.delete(next);
      if (this.down.size === 0) this.dir = this.up.size ? "UP" : "IDLE";
    }
  }
  cost(floor: number, dir: Dir) { return Math.abs(this.floor - floor) + (this.up.size + this.down.size) * 2; }
}
```

Spreading a floor onto Math.min is fine for small N; use a sorted structure for large buildings.

## Dispatch strategy

The controller scores each elevator for a hall call and picks the lowest cost. Reasonable rules:
- Idle car: cost is distance.
- Car moving toward the caller in the same direction: cost is distance (it passes the floor anyway).
- Car moving away: cost is distance to its farthest stop plus the way back.

Put the scoring behind `DispatchStrategy` so you can swap nearest-car for zoned or load-balanced policies.

```ts
interface DispatchStrategy { pick(cars: Elevator[], floor: number, dir: Dir): Elevator }
```

## State machine
States: IDLE, MOVING_UP, MOVING_DOWN, DOORS_OPEN (plus MAINTENANCE/EMERGENCY). Events: request, arrive, door timeout, overload. Using the State pattern keeps each state's behaviour in one class instead of nested ifs. Doors open on arrival, stay for a fixed time (a few seconds), then close and the next direction is chosen.

## Extensions
- Weight limit: reject further stops when overloaded, keep the door open.
- Express/service elevators: filter cars by capability inside the dispatch strategy.
- Peak mode (morning, send idle cars to lobby): a controller policy.
- Concurrency: requests arrive from many floors while cars move; protect the stop sets with a single event loop or a mutex per elevator.

## Pitfalls
- One global queue with FCFS ordering.
- Forgetting direction on hall calls: a car going up should not stop for a down request unless it is the farthest stop.
- Coupling movement to wall-clock time; drive it with `step()` so tests are deterministic.

