## Problem

Build a fixed-capacity cache where `get` and `put` run in O(1). When full, evict according to a policy. Two classic policies: LRU (least recently used) and LFU (least frequently used).

## Why naive approaches fail
A hash map gives O(1) lookup but no ordering. A list gives ordering but O(n) lookup. The textbook LRU combines a hash map with a doubly linked list: the map points at list nodes, moving a node to the head on access is O(1), and the tail is the eviction victim.

## The JavaScript shortcut
`Map` iterates keys in insertion order, and deleting then re-inserting a key moves it to the end. So the Map itself is the recency list: the first key is the least recently used, the last is the most recent.

```ts
class LRUCache<K, V> {
  private map = new Map<K, V>();
  constructor(private capacity: number) {
    if (capacity < 1) throw new Error("capacity must be >= 1");
  }
  get(key: K): V | undefined {
    if (!this.map.has(key)) return undefined;
    const v = this.map.get(key)!;
    this.map.delete(key);
    this.map.set(key, v);           // now most recent
    return v;
  }
  put(key: K, value: V): void {
    if (this.map.has(key)) this.map.delete(key);
    else if (this.map.size >= this.capacity) {
      const oldest = this.map.keys().next().value as K;
      this.map.delete(oldest);       // evict LRU
    }
    this.map.set(key, value);
  }
}
```

Both operations are amortised O(1) in V8. In an interview, say you know the hash map plus doubly linked list version (and sketch it) but would use Map in production JS for brevity. Be careful: `undefined` as a stored value is ambiguous with a miss; use `has`.

## LFU in O(1)

Track how often each key is used and evict the least frequent; break ties by least recent.

Structures:
- `values: Map<K, V>` and `freq: Map<K, number>`.
- `buckets: Map<number, Set<K>>` (a Set keeps insertion order, so the first element is the oldest at that frequency).
- `minFreq: number`.

```ts
class LFUCache<K, V> {
  private vals = new Map<K, V>();
  private freq = new Map<K, number>();
  private buckets = new Map<number, Set<K>>();
  private minFreq = 0;
  constructor(private capacity: number) {}

  private touch(key: K) {
    const f = this.freq.get(key)!;
    const b = this.buckets.get(f)!;
    b.delete(key);
    if (b.size === 0) { this.buckets.delete(f); if (this.minFreq === f) this.minFreq++; }
    this.freq.set(key, f + 1);
    if (!this.buckets.has(f + 1)) this.buckets.set(f + 1, new Set());
    this.buckets.get(f + 1)!.add(key);
  }
  get(key: K) {
    if (!this.vals.has(key)) return undefined;
    this.touch(key);
    return this.vals.get(key);
  }
  put(key: K, value: V) {
    if (this.capacity === 0) return;
    if (this.vals.has(key)) { this.vals.set(key, value); this.touch(key); return; }
    if (this.vals.size >= this.capacity) {
      const bucket = this.buckets.get(this.minFreq)!;
      const victim = bucket.values().next().value as K;
      bucket.delete(victim);
      if (bucket.size === 0) this.buckets.delete(this.minFreq);
      this.vals.delete(victim); this.freq.delete(victim);
    }
    this.vals.set(key, value); this.freq.set(key, 1);
    if (!this.buckets.has(1)) this.buckets.set(1, new Set());
    this.buckets.get(1)!.add(key);
    this.minFreq = 1;
  }
}
```

## Choosing a policy
- LRU: good default for temporal locality (recent things are reused).
- LFU: better when a stable set of hot keys exists, but old popular keys linger after they stop being hot (frequency pollution); add ageing/decay if needed.
- Real systems use approximations: Redis samples keys rather than tracking exact LRU.

## Extensions
- TTL: store `expiresAt` per entry; expire lazily on `get` and sweep periodically.
- Thread safety: see the concurrency lesson; in one JS process these methods are atomic, but async loaders can cause duplicate fetches.
- Generic `Cache<K,V>` interface with `EvictionPolicy` strategy to swap LRU/LFU/FIFO.

## Pitfalls
- Forgetting to update recency on `put` of an existing key.
- Not resetting `minFreq` to 1 on insert.
- Capacity 0 edge case.

lld-cache-ratelimiter:1.body:
## Why rate limit

To protect a service from abusive or buggy clients, share capacity fairly, and control cost. The limiter sits at the gateway or inside the service and answers "may this key (user, IP, API key) proceed now?" in microseconds, so it must be cheap.

Design a common interface so algorithms are interchangeable (Strategy):

```ts
interface RateLimiter { allow(key: string, now?: number): boolean }
```

## 1. Token bucket
A bucket holds up to `capacity` tokens and refills at `refillPerSec`. Each request consumes one token. Bursts of up to `capacity` are allowed; the long-run average is the refill rate. Stripe and AWS API Gateway use this shape.

```ts
class TokenBucket implements RateLimiter {
  private state = new Map<string, { tokens: number; last: number }>();
  constructor(private capacity: number, private refillPerSec: number) {}
  allow(key: string, now = Date.now()) {
    const s = this.state.get(key) ?? { tokens: this.capacity, last: now };
    s.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, s.tokens + ((now - s.last) / 1000) * this.refillPerSec);
    s.last = now;
    const ok = s.tokens >= 1;
    if (ok) s.tokens -= 1;
    this.state.set(key, s);
    return ok;
  }
}
```

State per key is two numbers, so 1 million keys is tens of MB. Lazy refill avoids timers. Passing `now` makes it testable.

## 2. Leaky bucket
Requests enter a queue of fixed size and are processed at a constant rate; overflow is dropped. Output is perfectly smooth, which suits protecting a downstream with fixed throughput. Downside: a burst fills the queue and delays newer requests, and queue memory per key is higher. A common variant is the "meter": a counter that drains at a fixed rate and rejects when it exceeds capacity (equivalent to a token bucket mirrored).

## 3. Fixed window counter
Count requests per calendar window (for example per minute). Simple and O(1), but boundary bursts: 100 requests at 12:00:59 and 100 at 12:01:00 yield 200 in two seconds against a limit of 100/minute.

## 4. Sliding window log
Store a timestamp per request; on each call drop entries older than the window and allow if count is below the limit. Exact, but memory is O(limit) per key (a limit of 1,000/hour means up to 1,000 timestamps per user).

```ts
class SlidingLog implements RateLimiter {
  private logs = new Map<string, number[]>();
  constructor(private limit: number, private windowMs: number) {}
  allow(key: string, now = Date.now()) {
    const log = (this.logs.get(key) ?? []).filter((t) => t > now - this.windowMs);
    const ok = log.length < this.limit;
    if (ok) log.push(now);
    this.logs.set(key, log);
    return ok;
  }
}
```

## 5. Sliding window counter
Keep counts for the current and previous fixed windows and estimate: `prev * (1 - elapsedFraction) + current`. Two numbers per key, no boundary spike, and an error that is small if traffic is roughly uniform (Cloudflare reported a very low error rate in practice). Reject if the estimate is at or above the limit.

## Choosing
- Need bursts allowed with a steady average: token bucket.
- Need constant outflow to a fragile backend: leaky bucket.
- Need exact limits and low volume per key: sliding log.
- Need cheap and good enough at huge scale: sliding window counter.

## Distributed notes
In a cluster, per-key state lives in a shared store such as Redis, updated atomically with a Lua script or `INCR` + `EXPIRE`. Alternatives: sticky routing per key, or local limiters with a small shared sync (accept some over-admission). Return `Retry-After` and `X-RateLimit-Remaining` headers; respond 429.

## Pitfalls
- Using `setInterval` to refill every bucket.
- Unbounded key maps; evict idle keys.
- Clock skew across nodes; prefer the store's clock.
- Failing closed vs open when the limiter store is down: decide explicitly (usually fail open for non-critical APIs).

lld-cache-ratelimiter:2.body:
## What concurrency means in Node

Node executes your JavaScript on one thread. A synchronous function runs start to finish without interleaving, so `limiter.allow()` in the previous lesson is already atomic. Races appear when you `await`: between the await and its continuation, other requests run. Worker threads and multiple processes add true parallelism, and then in-memory state is not shared at all.

## The classic bug: check-then-act across an await

```ts
async function withdraw(id: string, amount: number) {
  const bal = await db.getBalance(id);      // A reads 100
  if (bal < amount) throw new Error("NSF");
  await db.setBalance(id, bal - amount);    // A writes 50; B also read 100 and writes 50
}
```
Two concurrent calls both read 100 and both withdraw 50: the balance ends at 50 instead of 0. The same shape breaks token buckets backed by Redis, seat reservations and inventory counters.

## Fix 1: keep check and update synchronous
If the state is in memory, read and write with no await between them. Do the async work before or after, not between.

## Fix 2: serialise with a mutex
A tiny promise-chain mutex gives per-key exclusion:

```ts
class Mutex {
  private tail: Promise<void> = Promise.resolve();
  lock(): Promise<() => void> {
    let release!: () => void;
    const next = new Promise<void>((r) => (release = r));
    const acquired = this.tail.then(() => release);
    this.tail = this.tail.then(() => next);
    return acquired;
  }
}

const locks = new Map<string, Mutex>();
async function withLock<T>(key: string, fn: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T> {
  const m = locks.get(key) ?? new Mutex();
  locks.set(key, m);
  const release = await m.lock();
  try { return await fn(); } finally { release(); }
}
```
Always release in `finally`. Clean up idle keys or the lock map leaks. This only works within one process.

## Fix 3: push atomicity to the store
Across processes use the database: `UPDATE accounts SET bal = bal - 50 WHERE id = ? AND bal >= 50`, a Redis Lua script or `INCR`, or optimistic concurrency (version column, retry on mismatch). This is the answer that holds when you scale to many instances.

## Cache stampede and single-flight
When a hot key expires, 1,000 concurrent requests all miss and hit the database. Cache the in-flight promise instead of the value:

```ts
class SingleFlightCache<V> {
  private data = new Map<string, V>();
  private inflight = new Map<string, Promise<V>>();
  async get(key: string, load: () => Promise<V>): Promise<V> {
    if (this.data.has(key)) return this.data.get(key)!;
    let p = this.inflight.get(key);
    if (!p) {
      p = load().then((v) => { this.data.set(key, v); return v; })
                .finally(() => this.inflight.delete(key));
      this.inflight.set(key, p);
    }
    return p;
  }
}
```
All callers share one load. Failures are not cached because the in-flight entry is removed in `finally` and data is only set on success. Add jittered TTLs so keys do not expire together.

## Applying it to the limiter
Rate limiter in one process: synchronous `allow` needs no lock. With Redis: run the refill-and-consume logic in one Lua script so it is atomic. For LRU with async loaders: single-flight plus synchronous Map updates.

## Pitfalls
- Assuming "single-threaded" means "no races".
- Holding a lock during slow I/O, which serialises everything; keep critical sections short.
- Deadlocks when locking multiple keys in inconsistent order; sort keys before locking.
- In-process locks in a multi-instance deployment give a false sense of safety.

lld-booking-splitwise:1.body:
## Problem

In a group, many expenses are shared: A pays for dinner, B pays for the cab. You record who paid and who owes what. "Simplify debts" should produce the fewest practical payments so everyone is settled.

## Model
```ts
interface Expense { paidBy: string; amount: number; splits: Map<string, number> } // cents
class BalanceSheet {
  private net = new Map<string, number>(); // + is owed to you, - is what you owe
  add(e: Expense) {
    this.bump(e.paidBy, e.amount);
    for (const [user, share] of e.splits) this.bump(user, -share);
  }
  private bump(u: string, d: number) { this.net.set(u, (this.net.get(u) ?? 0) + d); }
}
```
The sum of all net balances is always 0. That invariant is a great assertion in tests.

## Why simplification works
Raw pairwise debts can form chains and cycles: A owes B 10, B owes C 10. Net-wise A owes C 10 and B owes nothing. Reducing the debt graph to net balances erases who paid for what and keeps only each person's total position. You then need transfers from debtors to creditors.

## Greedy algorithm
1. Compute net balance per person.
2. Put creditors (net > 0) in a max-heap, debtors (net < 0, stored positive) in a max-heap.
3. Pop the largest of each. Transfer `t = min(credit, debt)` from debtor to creditor.
4. Push back whichever side still has a remainder. Repeat until empty.

```ts
function simplify(net: Map<string, number>) {
  const cr: [string, number][] = [], db: [string, number][] = [];
  for (const [u, v] of net) (v > 0 ? cr : v < 0 ? db : []).push([u, Math.abs(v)]);
  const out: { from: string; to: string; amount: number }[] = [];
  const byAmt = (a: [string, number], b: [string, number]) => b[1] - a[1];
  while (cr.length && db.length) {
    cr.sort(byAmt); db.sort(byAmt);        // use a real heap for large n
    const [c, d] = [cr[0], db[0]];
    const t = Math.min(c[1], d[1]);
    out.push({ from: d[0], to: c[0], amount: t });
    c[1] -= t; d[1] -= t;
    if (c[1] === 0) cr.shift();
    if (d[1] === 0) db.shift();
  }
  return out;
}
```
Each step settles at least one person, so there are at most n-1 transfers; with heaps the cost is O(n log n). Sorting each iteration (as written) is O(n^2 log n), fine for small groups; mention the heap version.

Example: A +50, B +10, C -30, D -30. Largest creditor A (50) with debtor C (30): C pays A 30; then A (20) with D (30): D pays A 20; then B (10) with D (10): D pays B 10. Three transfers for four people.

## Is greedy optimal?
Not always. The true minimum number of transfers is the number of people minus the maximum number of disjoint zero-sum subgroups, which is NP-hard in general (related to partition). For groups of 5-20 people, a brute-force/bitmask search over subsets is possible (O(3^n)) if the interviewer pushes. State that greedy is at most n-1 and usually close.

## Money correctness
- Store integer cents. 10.00 / 3 does not divide evenly; give each person floor(1000/3) = 333 and distribute the 1 leftover cent deterministically (for example to the payer or in id order). The sum of splits must equal the total exactly.
- Split types behind a strategy: EQUAL, EXACT, PERCENT, SHARES. Validate that exact amounts and percentages sum correctly before accepting.
- Currency conversion changes the model: keep balances per currency.

## Extensions
- Settle-up records as negative expenses.
- Per-group vs global balances.
- Audit trail: store expenses immutably and derive balances; recomputation is cheap.

## Pitfalls
- Floating point rounding leaving a balance of 0.0000001.
- Mutating the original expenses when simplifying; simplification is a view.
- Forgetting the zero-sum invariant check.

