## Problem

A checkout supports several payment methods. A first draft contains `if (method === "card") {...} else if (method === "upi") {...}` repeated in pay, refund and status code. Every new method edits and risks all of it. The Strategy pattern fixes this by moving each algorithm behind a common interface and choosing one at runtime.

## Design

```ts
interface PaymentResult { ok: boolean; txnId: string; reason?: string }

interface PaymentStrategy {
  readonly method: string;
  pay(orderId: string, amountMinor: number, idempotencyKey: string): Promise<PaymentResult>;
  refund(txnId: string, amountMinor: number): Promise<PaymentResult>;
}

class CardPayment implements PaymentStrategy {
  readonly method = "card";
  constructor(private gateway: CardGateway) {}
  async pay(orderId: string, amount: number, key: string) {
    const r = await this.gateway.charge({ orderId, amount }, key);
    return { ok: r.status === "succeeded", txnId: r.id, reason: r.error };
  }
  async refund(txnId: string, amount: number) { /* gateway.refund */ return { ok: true, txnId }; }
}

class PaymentRegistry {
  private map = new Map<string, PaymentStrategy>();
  register(s: PaymentStrategy) { this.map.set(s.method, s); }
  get(method: string) {
    const s = this.map.get(method);
    if (!s) throw new Error("UNSUPPORTED_METHOD");
    return s;
  }
}

class CheckoutService {
  constructor(private payments: PaymentRegistry, private orders: OrderRepo) {}
  async checkout(orderId: string, method: string) {
    const order = await this.orders.get(orderId);
    const res = await this.payments.get(method).pay(orderId, order.totalMinor, `pay-${orderId}`);
    await this.orders.markPaid(orderId, res);
    return res;
  }
}
```
`CheckoutService` depends on the abstraction; adding `WalletPayment` is one new class plus one `register` call (open/closed principle). Test with a `FakePayment`.

## Strategy vs Factory vs State
- Strategy: interchangeable algorithms chosen by the caller or config.
- Factory/Registry: how you obtain the right strategy.
- State: behaviour changes because the object's own state changes (order lifecycle), not because a caller picked it.

## Payment realities that interviewers like
- Idempotency: network timeouts happen after the charge may have succeeded. Send an idempotency key (derived from the order id) so a retry returns the original result instead of charging twice.
- States, not booleans: CREATED, PENDING, SUCCEEDED, FAILED, REFUNDED. Many methods (UPI, bank redirect) complete asynchronously through a webhook; the strategy may return PENDING and the final state arrives later.
- Webhook handling: verify the signature, be idempotent, and tolerate out-of-order events.
- Never log or store raw card numbers; use tokens from the gateway.
- Amount in integer minor units; currency attached to the amount.

## Extensions
- Split payments (part wallet, part card): a composite strategy that runs strategies in sequence and rolls back on failure.
- Retries/fallback gateway: a decorator strategy wrapping another with retry or failover.
- Fees: strategy exposes `fee(amount)`.

## Pitfalls
- Leaking vendor types (Stripe objects) through the interface.
- Branching on method in the service anyway ("if card then...").
- Treating a timeout as failure and retrying without an idempotency key.

lld-games:0.body:
## Problem

Design turn-based games such as tic-tac-toe, connect-four, snakes and ladders or chess. Interviewers use them to test clean separation of responsibilities and how easily you can add a variant (bigger board, a bot, a new rule).

## The loop in one picture
Repeat: current player proposes a move, the game validates it, applies it, checks for win or draw, then hands the turn to the next player. Everything else is detail.

## Classes

```ts
type Mark = "X" | "O";
type Result = { kind: "IN_PROGRESS" } | { kind: "WON"; winner: string } | { kind: "DRAW" };

interface Move { row: number; col: number }
interface Player { name: string; mark: Mark; nextMove(board: Board): Promise<Move> }

class Board {
  private cells: (Mark | null)[][];
  private filled = 0;
  constructor(readonly size: number) {
    this.cells = Array.from({ length: size }, () => Array<Mark | null>(size).fill(null));
  }
  isFree(m: Move) {
    return m.row >= 0 && m.row < this.size && m.col >= 0 && m.col < this.size && !this.cells[m.row][m.col];
  }
  place(m: Move, mark: Mark) { this.cells[m.row][m.col] = mark; this.filled++; }
  get full() { return this.filled === this.size * this.size; }
  at(r: number, c: number) { return this.cells[r][c]; }
}

class Game {
  private turn = 0;
  result: Result = { kind: "IN_PROGRESS" };
  constructor(private board: Board, private players: Player[], private rules: WinRule) {}

  async play() {
    while (this.result.kind === "IN_PROGRESS") {
      const p = this.players[this.turn % this.players.length];
      const m = await p.nextMove(this.board);
      if (!this.board.isFree(m)) continue;          // or re-prompt with an error
      this.board.place(m, p.mark);
      if (this.rules.hasWon(this.board, m, p.mark)) this.result = { kind: "WON", winner: p.name };
      else if (this.board.full) this.result = { kind: "DRAW" };
      else this.turn++;
    }
    return this.result;
  }
}
```

## O(1) win detection for tic-tac-toe
Instead of scanning the board, keep counters per row, column and both diagonals: add +1 for X and -1 for O at the move's row, column and (if on them) diagonals. If any counter reaches +n or -n, that player wins. Each move costs O(1) and memory is O(n).

```ts
class CounterRule { // implements WinRule for tic-tac-toe
  private rows: number[]; private cols: number[]; private d = 0; private a = 0;
  constructor(private n: number) { this.rows = Array(n).fill(0); this.cols = Array(n).fill(0); }
  hasWon(_b: Board, m: Move, mark: Mark) {
    const v = mark === "X" ? 1 : -1;
    this.rows[m.row] += v; this.cols[m.col] += v;
    if (m.row === m.col) this.d += v;
    if (m.row + m.col === this.n - 1) this.a += v;
    return [this.rows[m.row], this.cols[m.col], this.d, this.a].some((x) => Math.abs(x) === this.n);
  }
}
```
For connect-four or gomoku, scan outwards from the last move in 4 directions instead.

## Turn management
- Round-robin index modulo player count supports 2+ players (snakes and ladders).
- Skipped turns, extra turns (rolling a six) and eliminations: keep an `active` list and let a rule decide the next player.
- Time limits: wrap `nextMove` with a timeout and forfeit.

## Separating I/O
`Player.nextMove` is async so a console, a websocket client or a minimax bot all fit. Game never prints. A `GameListener` (observer) can render the board and announce results, which also enables spectators and replays.

## Undo and replay
Store the move list. Undo pops the last move and rebuilds or reverses it; replay re-applies moves from the start. This is the Command pattern: each move is an object with `apply` and `undo`.

## Pitfalls
- Scanning the whole board every move when only the last move can create a win.
- Mixing printing and input handling into the game logic.
- Hard-coding 3x3 and two players.
- Forgetting the draw condition, or checking draw before win on the final move.

