## Problem

Your tic-tac-toe works. Now the interviewer says: "Make the board 4x4. Add misere rules where completing a line loses. Add a bot." If rules are hard-coded in `Game`, each change edits the same class and risks regressions. Extract what varies.

## What varies in a game
- Move validation (what is a legal move?).
- Win/draw conditions.
- Who picks the next move (human, random bot, minimax).
- Scoring or special effects (snakes and ladders: snakes, ladders, extra turn on six).
- Lifecycle (waiting for players, running, finished).

## Strategy for rules

```ts
interface WinRule { evaluate(board: Board, last: Move, mark: Mark): Result }
interface MoveRule { check(board: Board, m: Move, mark: Mark): string | null } // error message or null

class Game {
  constructor(
    private board: Board,
    private win: WinRule,
    private moveRules: MoveRule[],
  ) {}

  tryMove(m: Move, mark: Mark) {
    for (const r of this.moveRules) {
      const err = r.check(this.board, m, mark);
      if (err) throw new Error(err);
    }
    this.board.place(m, mark);
    return this.win.evaluate(this.board, m, mark);
  }
}
```
Adding "no move in the centre on the first turn" is one new `MoveRule` in the list: no existing code edited (open/closed principle). Misere is a `WinRule` that inverts the outcome.

## Bots as strategies
```ts
interface MoveChooser { choose(board: Board, mark: Mark): Move }
class RandomBot implements MoveChooser { /* pick a random free cell */ }
class MinimaxBot implements MoveChooser { /* alpha-beta, depth-limited */ }
```
Minimax on 3x3 explores at most 9! = 362,880 move orderings (far fewer with pruning and memoisation), trivial. On bigger boards use depth limits and heuristics.

## State for the lifecycle
When actions are legal only in certain phases, the State pattern removes branching on a status enum:

```ts
interface GameState {
  join(g: GameSession, p: Player): void;
  move(g: GameSession, p: Player, m: Move): void;
}
class Waiting implements GameState {
  join(g: GameSession, p: Player) { g.players.push(p); if (g.players.length === 2) g.setState(new InProgress()); }
  move() { throw new Error("Game has not started"); }
}
class InProgress implements GameState {
  join() { throw new Error("Game is full"); }
  move(g: GameSession, p: Player, m: Move) { /* validate, apply, maybe g.setState(new Finished(result)) */ }
}
class Finished implements GameState {
  join() { throw new Error("Game over"); }
  move() { throw new Error("Game over"); }
}
```
Each state class holds only its own behaviour, and transitions are explicit calls to `setState`.

## Strategy vs State (frequent question)
- Strategy: the client picks one algorithm, it usually stays for the object's life, strategies do not know each other.
- State: the object switches behaviour itself as events occur; states often know their successors.
- Same UML shape, different intent.

## When not to use patterns
If there is one rule and no foreseeable variant, an `if` is fine. Introduce an interface at the second variant, not the first. Over-abstraction is an interview red flag.

## Pitfalls
- Strategy interfaces that need the whole Game as argument (tight coupling); pass only the board and move.
- Rule order dependence between MoveRules; document or make rules independent.
- State objects holding mutable shared data; keep data in the context (`GameSession`).

