## Problem

Build an in-memory message broker: publishers send messages to named topics; zero or more consumers subscribe to a topic and each gets every message. Producers and consumers should not know each other.

## Core model

```ts
type Handler<T> = (msg: T) => Promise<void> | void;

class Subscriber<T> {
  private queue: T[] = [];
  private running = false;
  constructor(readonly id: string, private handler: Handler<T>, private maxQueue = 1000) {}

  enqueue(msg: T): boolean {
    if (this.queue.length >= this.maxQueue) return false;   // overflow policy: reject
    this.queue.push(msg);
    void this.drain();
    return true;
  }
  private async drain() {
    if (this.running) return;           // one worker per subscriber preserves order
    this.running = true;
    while (this.queue.length) {
      const msg = this.queue.shift()!;
      try { await this.handler(msg); } catch (e) { /* retry or send to a dead-letter list */ }
    }
    this.running = false;
  }
}

class Topic<T> {
  private subs = new Map<string, Subscriber<T>>();
  constructor(readonly name: string) {}
  subscribe(s: Subscriber<T>) { this.subs.set(s.id, s); }
  unsubscribe(id: string) { this.subs.delete(id); }
  publish(msg: T) { for (const s of this.subs.values()) s.enqueue(msg); }
}

class Broker<T> {
  private topics = new Map<string, Topic<T>>();
  topic(name: string) {
    if (!this.topics.has(name)) this.topics.set(name, new Topic<T>(name));
    return this.topics.get(name)!;
  }
  publish(name: string, msg: T) { this.topic(name).publish(msg); }
  subscribe(name: string, id: string, h: Handler<T>) { this.topic(name).subscribe(new Subscriber(id, h)); }
}
```

Publishing is O(number of subscribers) and never waits for handlers. Per-subscriber queues mean a slow Email handler cannot block Billing.

## Ordering and delivery
- Order: one drain worker per subscriber gives FIFO per subscriber per topic. Parallel handlers lose ordering; if you need parallelism, partition by key (hash(key) mod P) so messages of one key stay ordered, as Kafka does.
- Semantics: the code above is at-most-once; a crash loses queued messages. At-least-once needs a message stored until the handler acks, with retries (and consumers must be idempotent). Exactly-once is generally achieved by at-least-once plus idempotent processing, not by magic.
- Retries: exponential backoff with a retry cap, then move to a dead-letter queue instead of looping forever.

## Backpressure
Choose what happens when a subscriber's queue is full: reject the new message (publisher gets an error), drop oldest, or block the publisher. Dropping suits metrics; blocking suits payments. Always make it a configurable policy and expose the queue depth as a metric.

## Queue vs pub-sub
- Queue (competing consumers): each message goes to one of N workers; used for load sharing.
- Pub-sub: each message goes to every subscriber; used for events.
- Consumer groups combine them: every group receives each message, and inside a group only one member handles it.

## Pull model and offsets
Instead of pushing, store messages in an append-only log per topic and let each subscriber keep an offset. A subscriber reads from its offset, acks by advancing it, and can rewind to replay. Memory then depends on retention, not on the number of slow consumers. This is the Kafka design and a good extension to mention.

## Extensions
- Wildcard topics (`orders.*`) via a trie or regex match.
- Message filtering by attributes.
- Persistence via a write-ahead log and snapshot.
- Observer pattern is the in-process analogue; the broker adds topics, queues and async delivery.

## Pitfalls
- Awaiting all handlers inside `publish`, coupling producers to consumers' speed.
- Unbounded queues leading to memory exhaustion.
- Mutating a shared message object that multiple subscribers receive; freeze or clone it.
- Handler exceptions that kill the drain loop (the try/catch above prevents it).

