## Problem

Design a scheduler: `schedule(fn, delayMs)` runs `fn` once after a delay; optionally support recurring tasks and cancellation. Think of a simplified Quartz or a job queue's delayed jobs.

## Why not setTimeout per task
`setTimeout` works for small numbers, but a million timers means a million timer objects, no central view for cancellation, listing or metrics, and nothing survives a restart. A heap-based scheduler keeps tasks as data and needs only one live timer.

## Data structure: min-heap
A binary min-heap keyed by `runAt`: push O(log n), pop O(log n), peek O(1). Break ties with an increasing sequence number so same-time tasks run in insertion order.

```ts
interface Task { id: number; runAt: number; fn: () => Promise<void> | void; every?: number; cancelled?: boolean }

class MinHeap<T> {
  private a: T[] = [];
  constructor(private lt: (x: T, y: T) => boolean) {}
  get size() { return this.a.length; }
  peek() { return this.a[0]; }
  push(v: T) {
    this.a.push(v);
    let i = this.a.length - 1;
    while (i > 0) {
      const p = (i - 1) >> 1;
      if (!this.lt(this.a[i], this.a[p])) break;
      [this.a[i], this.a[p]] = [this.a[p], this.a[i]]; i = p;
    }
  }
  pop() {
    const top = this.a[0], last = this.a.pop()!;
    if (this.a.length) {
      this.a[0] = last;
      let i = 0;
      for (;;) {
        const l = 2 * i + 1, r = l + 1; let m = i;
        if (l < this.a.length && this.lt(this.a[l], this.a[m])) m = l;
        if (r < this.a.length && this.lt(this.a[r], this.a[m])) m = r;
        if (m === i) break;
        [this.a[i], this.a[m]] = [this.a[m], this.a[i]]; i = m;
      }
    }
    return top;
  }
}

class Scheduler {
  private heap = new MinHeap<Task>((x, y) => x.runAt < y.runAt || (x.runAt === y.runAt && x.id < y.id));
  private tasks = new Map<number, Task>();
  private timer?: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>;
  private seq = 0;

  schedule(fn: Task["fn"], delayMs: number, every?: number) {
    const t: Task = { id: ++this.seq, runAt: Date.now() + delayMs, fn, every };
    this.tasks.set(t.id, t); this.heap.push(t); this.arm();
    return t.id;
  }
  cancel(id: number) { const t = this.tasks.get(id); if (t) { t.cancelled = true; this.tasks.delete(id); } }

  private arm() {
    clearTimeout(this.timer);
    const top = this.heap.peek();
    if (!top) return;
    this.timer = setTimeout(() => void this.tick(), Math.max(0, top.runAt - Date.now()));
  }
  private async tick() {
    const now = Date.now();
    while (this.heap.size && this.heap.peek().runAt <= now) {
      const t = this.heap.pop();
      if (t.cancelled) continue;
      try { await t.fn(); } catch { /* log, maybe retry */ }
      if (t.every && !t.cancelled) { t.runAt = Date.now() + t.every; this.heap.push(t); } else this.tasks.delete(t.id);
    }
    this.arm();
  }
}
```
Cancellation is lazy: mark and skip on pop, which avoids an O(n) heap removal.

## Execution concerns
- Slow tasks: awaiting inside `tick` delays later tasks. For concurrency, run tasks without awaiting (bounded by a worker pool) and catch errors per task.
- Drift in recurring tasks: schedule next run from the planned time (`runAt + every`) for fixed-rate, or from completion for fixed-delay; say which.
- Node timers have a maximum delay of about 24.8 days (2^31-1 ms); longer delays fire immediately unless you re-arm in chunks.
- Retries with exponential backoff: re-push with a larger `runAt`.

## Scaling and durability
- A timing wheel (buckets per tick, hierarchical for long delays) gives O(1) insert and expiry, used in Kafka and Netty for huge numbers of timeouts.
- To survive restarts, persist tasks (database table indexed on `run_at`) and poll `WHERE run_at <= now() ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` so several workers can share the load without double execution.
- Distributed: a lease per task or a leader that polls; make tasks idempotent because at-least-once is the realistic guarantee.

## Pitfalls
- Using `setInterval` for recurring work, which overlaps if a run takes longer than the interval.
- Forgetting to re-arm the timer when a sooner task is added.
- Not catching exceptions, which would stop the scheduler loop.
- Using wall-clock time that can jump (NTP); prefer monotonic time (`performance.now()`) for in-process delays.

