## Problem

A vending machine or ATM behaves differently depending on where it is in a flow. Pressing "select item" with no money must be rejected; inserting a card after a session started must be ignored. The naive implementation keeps a `status` string and every method begins with a growing `switch (status)`. Add a state and you edit every method.

## State pattern

Define one interface with every event, one class per state. The machine (context) forwards calls to its current state object.

```ts
interface VendingState {
  insertMoney(m: VendingMachine, cents: number): void;
  selectItem(m: VendingMachine, code: string): void;
  cancel(m: VendingMachine): void;
  dispense(m: VendingMachine): void;
}

class VendingMachine {
  state: VendingState = new IdleState();
  balance = 0;
  selected?: string;
  constructor(readonly inventory: Inventory) {}
  setState(s: VendingState) { this.state = s; }
  insertMoney(c: number) { this.state.insertMoney(this, c); }
  selectItem(code: string) { this.state.selectItem(this, code); }
  cancel() { this.state.cancel(this); }
}

class IdleState implements VendingState {
  insertMoney(m: VendingMachine, c: number) { m.balance += c; m.setState(new HasMoneyState()); }
  selectItem() { throw new Error("Insert money first"); }
  cancel() { /* nothing to refund */ }
  dispense() { throw new Error("Nothing selected"); }
}

class HasMoneyState implements VendingState {
  insertMoney(m: VendingMachine, c: number) { m.balance += c; }
  selectItem(m: VendingMachine, code: string) {
    const item = m.inventory.peek(code);
    if (!item) throw new Error("Sold out or unknown");
    if (m.balance < item.priceCents) throw new Error("Insufficient funds");
    m.selected = code;
    m.setState(new DispensingState());
    m.state.dispense(m);
  }
  cancel(m: VendingMachine) { refund(m.balance); m.balance = 0; m.setState(new IdleState()); }
  dispense() { throw new Error("Select an item first"); }
}

class DispensingState implements VendingState {
  insertMoney() { throw new Error("Please wait"); }
  selectItem() { throw new Error("Please wait"); }
  cancel() { throw new Error("Cannot cancel while dispensing"); }
  dispense(m: VendingMachine) {
    const item = m.inventory.take(m.selected!);
    const change = m.balance - item.priceCents;
    if (change > 0) refund(change);
    m.balance = 0; m.selected = undefined;
    m.setState(new IdleState());
  }
}
declare function refund(cents: number): void;
```

## Why it is better
- Open/closed: adding `MaintenanceState` means one new class; existing states stay unchanged (though the interface may need a new method for a new event).
- Illegal actions are impossible by construction: each state decides what it allows.
- Each class is small and testable.

## Trade-offs
- More classes for tiny machines; with 3 states and 3 events a table-driven FSM (`Record<State, Record<Event, State>>`) is lighter.
- Behaviour that is identical across states is duplicated; use a base class with defaults that throw "not allowed".
- Transitions are scattered across state classes; keep a state diagram in the docs.

## ATM as the same pattern
States: Idle, CardInserted, PinVerified, TransactionSelected, Dispensing. Events: insertCard, enterPin, selectOperation, withdraw, ejectCard. After three wrong PINs the card is retained (transition to a terminal state). The ATM context holds the card and session; bank access sits behind an interface so the machine is testable.

## Interview extras
- Concurrency: one user at a time, so one session object; cash and inventory updates must be atomic with the transaction.
- Failure mid-dispense: a hardware fault after charging needs a compensating refund; log every transition.
- Strategy and State are structurally similar; here the object changes itself, so State is the right label.

## Pitfalls
- A `status` enum plus switch statements inside the state classes (defeats the point).
- States storing machine data instead of the context holding it.
- Forgetting the cancel path and refunds.

