## Problem

Design the core of a library system: members search the catalog, borrow and return books, reserve books that are out, and pay fines for late returns. Librarians add and remove books.

## Entities and the key modelling decision
- `Book`: ISBN, title, authors, category. Shared metadata.
- `BookItem`: one physical copy with a barcode and status (AVAILABLE, LOANED, RESERVED, LOST). A library may own 5 copies of one book.
- `Member`: id, name, tier, active loans.
- `Loan`: bookItem, member, borrowedAt, dueAt, returnedAt.
- `Reservation`: member, book, createdAt, status.
- `Catalog`: search by title, author, ISBN, category.
- `FinePolicy`, `LoanPolicy`: pricing and limits as interfaces.

Interviewers look for the Book vs BookItem split: reservations apply to a book (any copy), loans apply to a specific copy.

## Design

```ts
type ItemStatus = "AVAILABLE" | "LOANED" | "RESERVED" | "LOST";

class BookItem {
  status: ItemStatus = "AVAILABLE";
  constructor(readonly barcode: string, readonly isbn: string) {}
}

interface LoanPolicy { maxLoans(m: Member): number; loanDays(m: Member): number }
interface FinePolicy { fine(dueAt: Date, returnedAt: Date): number }

class PerDayFine implements FinePolicy {
  constructor(private centsPerDay: number) {}
  fine(due: Date, ret: Date) {
    const late = Math.ceil((ret.getTime() - due.getTime()) / 86_400_000);
    return late > 0 ? late * this.centsPerDay : 0;
  }
}

class Library {
  constructor(private items: Map<string, BookItem>, private loans: Loan[],
              private lp: LoanPolicy, private fp: FinePolicy, private now = () => new Date()) {}

  checkout(member: Member, barcode: string): Loan {
    const item = this.items.get(barcode);
    if (!item || item.status !== "AVAILABLE") throw new Error("NOT_AVAILABLE");
    if (member.activeLoans >= this.lp.maxLoans(member)) throw new Error("LIMIT_REACHED");
    if (member.unpaidFines > 0) throw new Error("PAY_FINES");
    item.status = "LOANED";                  // atomic with the checks above in one tick
    member.activeLoans++;
    const due = new Date(this.now().getTime() + this.lp.loanDays(member) * 86_400_000);
    const loan = new Loan(item, member, this.now(), due);
    this.loans.push(loan);
    return loan;
  }

  returnItem(loan: Loan): number {
    const fine = this.fp.fine(loan.dueAt, this.now());
    loan.returnedAt = this.now();
    loan.member.activeLoans--;
    loan.member.unpaidFines += fine;
    loan.item.status = this.hasReservation(loan.item.isbn) ? "RESERVED" : "AVAILABLE";
    return fine;
  }
  private hasReservation(isbn: string) { return false; /* check queue; notify first member */ }
}
```

## Reservations
Keep a FIFO queue per ISBN. On return, if a reservation exists, set the copy to RESERVED for the first member, notify them (Observer: `NotificationService`) and expire the hold after a few days, passing it to the next member.

## Search
- A few thousand books: linear scan or `Map` indexes by ISBN.
- Larger: inverted indexes (token -> ISBN set) for title/author; in production, a search engine.
- Keep `Catalog` separate from `Library` so search can evolve independently.

## Rules and policies
- Different loan lengths and limits per tier (student 3 loans/14 days, staff 10 loans/30 days): `LoanPolicy` implementations.
- Renewals: allowed if no reservation exists and renewal count is under a cap.
- Lost book: charge a replacement fee, mark LOST.

## Concurrency
Two librarians checking out the same barcode at once: in a database, `UPDATE book_items SET status='LOANED' WHERE barcode=? AND status='AVAILABLE'` and check one row changed. See the booking lesson for the general pattern.

## Pitfalls
- Modelling only Book with a copy count; you lose which copy is lost or damaged.
- Embedding fine math inside Loan.
- Using the real clock directly, making overdue tests impossible.
- Letting members borrow while owing fines when the requirements say otherwise (clarify).

